to get delegated.

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:06.370
And then you have to do things
like monitor for compliance,

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.610
et cetera, which can be painful.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:11.540
We have purposely designed this
to be a very macro clustering

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:14.150
posture that you can implement,
which the IAM administrators

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:14.990
cannot override.

00:13:14.990 --> 00:13:17.090
So its a great way to
implement your sort

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:20.390
of mandatory minimum
security controls, right?

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:23.720
And then you can have further
fine-grained, application

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:27.230
specific controls
defined through IAM.

00:13:31.150 --> 00:13:33.650
So we talked about
data exfiltration.

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.310
So it's important to understand
how can data exfiltration occur

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:37.930
from managed
services, and why that

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:41.650
is an issue on public clouds.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:43.060
So typically what
would happen is

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:47.100
that your standard firewalls,
say your VPC network firewall,

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:52.240
you'd set a policy like
allow all access to GCS.

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:55.500
So it would be like
storage.google.googleapis.com

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:57.160
for example, right.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.830
What that means is,
you can not only

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.170
access your own buckets
you can pretty much access

00:14:02.170 --> 00:14:03.100
any buckets, right?

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:06.340
Because your firewall is
not aware of the resource

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:10.772
model of GCS up on cloud, or
BigQuery, or for that matter

00:14:10.772 --> 00:14:12.730
any of the services that
you are consuming that

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:14.050
are fully managed.

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:18.610
So that leads to certain
risks that you are exposed to.

00:14:18.610 --> 00:14:20.800
The first is that
your IAM become

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.450
sort of a single point of
failure for your security.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.380
So you have-- if you have
misconfigured IAM policies,

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:28.600
and they may be intentional
or for malicious reasons,

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:30.750
or just for convenience
reasons, right,

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.550
you end up accidentally
exposing data.

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:36.000
And this has been fairly--

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.470
its been in the
news a lot, right?

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:41.790
Like this particular S3 bucket
or something that got exposed.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.820
And its fundamentally a
lot of that, if you go

00:14:45.820 --> 00:14:47.740
back and look at the
cause, its the first one.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:50.230
But then that's
not the only thing.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.020
You can have somebody
stealing identities.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:54.880
And phishing and
stolen identities

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:56.620
are fairly common, right?

00:14:56.620 --> 00:14:59.830
We talk elsewhere about how to
prevent your identities being

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:04.300
stolen using UB keys and
our phishing capabilities.

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:06.790
But nevertheless, you
still want to assume

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:10.780
that an entity has been phished
or the token has been stolen.

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:13.806
In which case, somebody
could access the data

00:15:13.806 --> 00:15:15.430
from the internet
pretty much, and then

00:15:15.430 --> 00:15:17.500
drop it-- push it
to Dropbox, right?

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:18.690
And that's an exfiltration.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.997
The third is you actually
have an insider who,

00:15:21.997 --> 00:15:23.830
and this is one of the
more difficult things

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:24.560
to control for.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.410
So here you can just
mitigate the risk

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.540
versus eliminate the risk
in this particular case.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:30.586
But you can mitigate
it significantly.

00:15:30.586 --> 00:15:33.220
Is they are either
using compromised code

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.130
running in a VM or directly
accessing the bucket themselves

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:40.300
and running as simple as
a GCS dot copy command.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:44.140
That's all you need right,
to move pretty much terabytes

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:46.710
of data from
buckets that you own

00:15:46.710 --> 00:15:49.090
to public buckets
or buckets that

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.180
belong to the attacker, right?

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:54.520
So these are the paths
that we have shown here,

00:15:54.520 --> 00:15:58.020
which are exposed to the risk.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.580
So effectively what VPC
service controls does, is

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:03.760
it creates a virtual
perimeter around the resources

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:06.160
of your managed services
like GCS buckets or BigQuery

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:11.200
datasets, and it then
also maps the perimeter

00:16:11.200 --> 00:16:13.260
to one or more VPC networks.

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.699
So you can simply add
your host project value,

00:16:15.699 --> 00:16:17.990
if your PC network is defined,
into the virtual service

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:18.790
parameter.

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:20.720
And therefore, all
VMs in that network

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:24.400
then have access to the
resources within the perimeter,

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.690
within the service perimeter.

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:29.530
But the VMs don't have
access to resources

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:31.160
that are outside the perimeter.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:32.890
So even if you read
the data into a VM,

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:36.340
you cannot write the data
to a resource that's outside

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:37.330
the perimeter.

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:41.470
Further, your service to service
communications, like copy calls

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:43.990
from say from GCS copy
call example that I gave,

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.690
or BigQuery trying to
query a GCS bucket.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:50.140
These kind of pathways are also
controlled by this perimeter.

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:53.080
So what it does for
you, is it allows

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:55.240
you to effectively
take your data that's

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:56.950
sitting in a fully
managed service

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:58.420
and make it private, right?

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:01.830
And it provides in that sense
an ingress control, right?

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:03.330
Controls accessed
from the internet.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:06.839
So access from the internet
is denied by default.

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:07.760
And egress controls.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.160
So you prevent the
copying of data

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:13.030
outside to
unauthorized projects.

00:17:13.030 --> 00:17:16.510
Ines did cover this, so I'll
go through this fairly quickly.

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:19.630
You can extend the security
posture to your on-prem

00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:22.480
if you're connected to a
VPN or private interconnect.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.910
You're on-prem
machines also will not

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.560
be able to-- if
you connect over--

00:17:26.560 --> 00:17:29.770
if you use this technology and
you access our data privately,

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:32.060
you will also not be able
to, even from on-premise,

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:34.930
not be able to access
unauthorized GCS resources

00:17:34.930 --> 00:17:35.740
or buckets.

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:38.470
So your exfiltration control
is propagated all the way

00:17:38.470 --> 00:17:40.120
to on-premise.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:42.590
And lastly, if you do
want to open up access,

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:45.160
you can do things like only
allow access from an IP range.

00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:47.070
That's sort of the
LT environment.

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:49.660
But you can do something more
sophisticated like I would only

00:17:49.660 --> 00:17:52.950
allow access from a
managed device given

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:56.087
you know UB key touch, and
so on and so forth, right.

00:17:56.087 --> 00:17:57.670
So that gives you
the full the context

00:17:57.670 --> 00:18:00.280
of our access to the parameter.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:05.020
With that, I'll show
you how you can easily

00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:08.410
define these policies and
manage them centrally at scale.

00:18:08.410 --> 00:18:09.190
Demo please.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:12.600
Great.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:16.590
So this product is under
a private beta right now.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:19.020
So if you're white listed
at this point in time,

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:21.570
or if you wait for us to
be in the public beta,

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:25.050
in one of those cases you would
see a couple of menu items.

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:27.060
One that says VPC
service controls.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.440
The other says access
context manager.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.740
So access context
manager is the tool

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:34.320
that allows you to define
these sort of access levels

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.140
that gives you
context of our access.

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:38.347
But today we'll do a
quick demo of VPC service

00:18:38.347 --> 00:18:39.930
controls, the core
product that allows

00:18:39.930 --> 00:18:41.980
you to build a perimeter.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:47.030
So in this world, what
we're going to have is--

00:18:47.030 --> 00:18:49.210
I'm going to show you
while starting up,

00:18:49.210 --> 00:18:50.810
I'll start building
a new perimeter,

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:54.060
and I'll show you the structure
of the projects that exist.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:55.880
So let's just call
it prod perimeter.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.615
And let's look at the projects.

00:18:57.615 --> 00:19:03.260
So in the projects, I'm just
go to filter it by over here

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.030
so we can narrow down
to the projects that

00:19:05.030 --> 00:19:06.600
are relevant to this demo.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:08.570
So I've get an attacker
project to represent

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:12.240
the unauthorized project
that has GCS bucket in it.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.390
We also have a
compute VM, which I'm

00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:16.670
going to use to explain
how I'm accessing data

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:19.160
from within the network,
for example, right?

00:19:19.160 --> 00:19:22.130
And a storage project that
actually has the bucket

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:23.916
containing PII, right?

00:19:23.916 --> 00:19:25.290
So before I create
the perimeter,

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:28.870
I just wanted to show you a
quick exfiltration batch here.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:31.016
I hope you can hear me
when I keep the mic down.

00:19:43.491 --> 00:19:46.000
Sorry about that.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:46.650
There you go.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:50.770
So the one I have got in my left
hand side menu is on my laptop.

00:19:50.770 --> 00:19:53.561
So think of it as applying the
connects from the Internet,

00:19:53.561 --> 00:19:54.060
right?

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:56.470
On the right is I have
actually association

00:19:56.470 --> 00:19:59.850
to the VM that is inside the
production network, right?

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.059
So I'm going to
first demonstrate

00:20:02.059 --> 00:20:04.350
that this is my bucket that
is actually in a production

00:20:04.350 --> 00:20:06.830
bucket, WPSE secure bucket.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:10.180
And I am able to access
it from my laptop, which

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:11.890
is from the internet,
and I'm also

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:15.640
able to access it from my
production my environment.

00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:16.440
And I can then--

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:20.070
I have my attacker
bucket which obviously I

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:23.100
should be able to also be able
to access from the internet,

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:23.640
right?

00:20:23.640 --> 00:20:25.454
It got new data in it.

00:20:25.454 --> 00:20:28.240
And here's the
issue which we have,

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:33.750
which is you can also access
it from the production

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:35.400
environment, right?

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:40.130
So here's the exfiltration
but it's very complicated

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:41.550
as you will see.

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:46.050
It's the CB command that I run
over here from a production

00:20:46.050 --> 00:20:47.740
environment, and I'm done.

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:51.000
And now, let's look
at the attacker bucket

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:52.709
from the Internet.

00:20:52.709 --> 00:20:53.250
There you go.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.470
My PIIs out on the Internet.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.790
That's all I needed to do.

00:20:57.790 --> 00:21:00.300
And it's available now
with no IAM permissions,

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:02.450
because the attacker
bucket is a public bucket.

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:04.991
So your data is now exposed to
the Internet but just this one

00:21:04.991 --> 00:21:05.790
CP command.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:09.120
Now this is-- because
it's so simple you

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:11.220
do want to mitigate this risk.

00:21:11.220 --> 00:21:13.770
And the way we
mitigate this risk--

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:18.080
I'm going to quickly
delete the data for--

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:31.480
sorry I just deleted the data
for now from the record bucket.

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:35.002
But now we'll set up
the actual perimeter.

00:21:35.002 --> 00:21:37.210
So we are now going to add
the projects, which I just

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:39.420
showed you except for
the attacker buckets.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.430
We will add the
other buckets into--

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:45.305
all the other projects into a
service perimeter which I've

00:21:45.305 --> 00:21:46.736
called prod perimeter here.

00:21:46.736 --> 00:21:48.960
So we'll say add all
of three projects.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:51.040
So as you can see I don't
have to do anything GCS

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:52.660
specific, or BigQuery specific.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:56.080
This is one of the
powers of this control

00:21:56.080 --> 00:22:00.670
is that it allows you to have
a very homogeneous way of doing

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:02.540
this across different
service types.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:05.730
Now, I say which services
do I want to protect here.

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:07.666
Let's say I want to
protect the GCS API,

00:22:07.666 --> 00:22:09.415
or the cloud storage
API and the big table

00:22:09.415 --> 00:22:12.040
API because those are the
services that I care about.

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:13.840
So I'm going to
protect those too.

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:15.170
I don't use an access level.

00:22:15.170 --> 00:22:16.420
When I don't use an
access level, that

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:18.378
means there's no access
from the Internet i.e.,

00:22:18.378 --> 00:22:19.720
the data is private.

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:21.290
So let's do that.

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:22.210
Let's save it.

00:22:25.230 --> 00:22:26.960
And there you go.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.380
It's now-- it's created a
prod perimeter as you can see.

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:32.780
And it has the two
projects in there

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:35.219
and the two services
that it's protecting.

00:22:35.219 --> 00:22:37.260
So let's go back sometimes
it takes a little time

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:39.570
to roll out the
configuration because there's

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:40.770
a global configuration.

00:22:40.770 --> 00:22:44.215
But let's give it a quick
try to see if it's in place.

00:22:44.215 --> 00:22:45.840
So now let's run the
copy command again

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:47.970
and let's see what happens.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:50.670
The access was denied because
you could no longer exfiltrate

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:51.420
the data.

00:22:51.420 --> 00:22:56.220
And similarly, if you tried to
now access the private bucket

00:22:56.220 --> 00:22:58.500
from the internet,
right, you will

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.660
be denied access to the
private bucket from there.

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:02.610
So now you have
segmented your access.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:05.580
Your internet facing client
can access the attacker bucket

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:07.740
but not the secure bucket.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.680
And your production system
can access the secure bucket

00:23:10.680 --> 00:23:13.070
but not the attacker, right?

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:16.530
So it's now fully segmented
and your exfiltration parts

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:18.570
have been closed up, right?

00:23:18.570 --> 00:23:22.427
You can then grant access pretty
simply using access context

00:23:22.427 --> 00:23:24.760
manager if you wanted to grant
access from the Internet,

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:26.910
so you can define
different access levels.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:30.660
And then, when you go back to
your service controls page,

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.520
you can go back
to your perimeter

00:23:32.520 --> 00:23:35.330
and you can simply decide
which access level do

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.280
you want to use to grant access
to resources in the service

00:23:38.280 --> 00:23:39.284
perimeter, right?

00:23:39.284 --> 00:23:40.950
So for example, I
could choose something

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:43.590
like high trust and [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:49.680
That means only clients that
are within this definition

00:23:49.680 --> 00:23:52.170
of high trust, which
is in this IP range,

00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:54.505
are allowed to access the data.

00:23:54.505 --> 00:23:55.380
That's it, thank you.

00:23:55.380 --> 00:23:58.670
Can we go back to
the slides please.

00:23:58.670 --> 00:24:03.921
And with that I would like to
invite on stage our colleagues

00:24:03.921 --> 00:24:04.420
from--

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:08.350
our customers Two
Sigma to talk about how

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:10.960
they have used this to mitigate
their exfiltration risk

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:13.482
in a real world deployment.

00:24:13.482 --> 00:24:16.368
