00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:01.780
we racked up.

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:03.530
But what we eventually
ended up going with

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:06.350
was creating home servers
in the GCP project

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:09.500
that were GCP instances,
with services accounts bound

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:10.310
to them.

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:12.200
And the role that the
service account had

00:27:12.200 --> 00:27:14.330
gave our researchers
and engineers

00:27:14.330 --> 00:27:17.450
the permissions they needed to
interact with BigQuery and GCS,

00:27:17.450 --> 00:27:19.130
and other core services.

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:22.160
The way they accessed those,
also from within Two Sigma,

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:25.839
also hand-waved a little is we
had a bastion type environment

00:27:25.839 --> 00:27:27.380
where we were able
to restrict things

00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:30.590
like copying and pasting, ACP,
but still give them shell level

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:34.730
access so that they could
then start to iterate.

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:36.426
One of the key
benefits here was they

00:27:36.426 --> 00:27:38.300
were able to start to
work on those data sets

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:40.042
and process them
and create research.

00:27:40.042 --> 00:27:42.500
And we were able to get them
up and running really quickly,

00:27:42.500 --> 00:27:44.676
but there were quite a
few drawbacks as well

00:27:44.676 --> 00:27:46.550
and I'll hand back to
Tony to talk about some

00:27:46.550 --> 00:27:47.258
of the drawbacks.

00:27:47.258 --> 00:27:48.380
TONY WALKER: Thanks, Nick.

00:27:48.380 --> 00:27:50.879
Yes so as Nick said, this got
us up and running very quickly

00:27:50.879 --> 00:27:52.150
but there were some downsides.

00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:56.070
So we start with
the home server.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.860
This was a real
disconnect for our users.

00:27:58.860 --> 00:28:00.540
Nick mentioned the
bastion access.

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:02.380
So this added additional hop.

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:04.260
A lot of the researchers
wanted to use tools

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:07.840
that they were used to using
home servers, inside Two Sigma.

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:10.070
But to actually get out
and play with the data

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:12.694
in a read-write manner they had
to go out onto the home servers

00:28:12.694 --> 00:28:13.696
to do this.

00:28:13.696 --> 00:28:15.070
This also, as Nick
mentioned, the

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:17.860
added complexity that we had
to deal with two different IAM

00:28:17.860 --> 00:28:18.470
identities.

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:21.272
We had to have a Two Sigma
user that was read only,

00:28:21.272 --> 00:28:24.186
and an external
identity that gave them

00:28:24.186 --> 00:28:26.360
the full read-write access.

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:28.430
Another big problem
was the fact that this

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:33.860
was connected over the
Internet and went by proxy.

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.586
So often one of the
common use cases

00:28:36.586 --> 00:28:38.210
the researchers would
have is that they

00:28:38.210 --> 00:28:42.366
would want to join the local Two
Sigma data with data that they

00:28:42.366 --> 00:28:43.550
had from the vendors.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:45.008
And there was no
good way form them

00:28:45.008 --> 00:28:47.270
to do this because essentially
this was exfiltration.

00:28:47.270 --> 00:28:49.890
So what they end up having
to do was raise a gira.

00:28:49.890 --> 00:28:51.670
They would put in a request.

00:28:51.670 --> 00:28:53.505
Their manager would
have to prove it.

00:28:53.505 --> 00:28:55.640
And then it would go
to Nick and his team,

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:58.470
and they would then have
to jump onto a secure site

00:28:58.470 --> 00:28:59.440
and push the data up.

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:03.220
And we're talking terabytes of
data, multiple times a week.

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:06.612
We ended up having to coalesce
those requests to once a week.

00:29:06.612 --> 00:29:08.570
And as you can imagine,
this was a real problem

00:29:08.570 --> 00:29:11.930
for trying to remain
agile for our teams.

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:13.070
Uploading was one problem.

00:29:13.070 --> 00:29:14.660
Downloading was also
another problem.

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:17.480
So the corporate
proxies we use these

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:22.005
to ensure that our security
perimeter is kept well

00:29:22.005 --> 00:29:24.490
put together for users
for Internet access.

00:29:24.490 --> 00:29:26.240
But when we had the
research teams wanting

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:30.152
to download terabytes of
data at the same time,

00:29:30.152 --> 00:29:32.360
we would often find that
they have either performance

00:29:32.360 --> 00:29:35.150
problems or actual
terminated jobs.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:36.650
And also other
engineering groups

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:38.720
completely, independent
from them in Two Sigma,

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:41.010
were suffering as well.

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:42.490
So these were some
real problems.

00:29:42.490 --> 00:29:43.906
And Nick's going
to talk about now

00:29:43.906 --> 00:29:45.410
about what we did to fix that.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:46.815
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS: Thank you.

00:29:46.815 --> 00:29:48.310
Being a design
partner with Google,

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:50.240
yeah we didn't want
to develop solutions

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:54.130
that were less than optimal or
would rack up even more tech

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:54.930
debt on our side.

00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:56.810
So we decided to
reach out to Google,

00:29:56.810 --> 00:29:59.660
and we engaged with the
network services team, Samrat

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:01.280
and Ines' team.

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:04.220
We talked about our requirements
and what our considerations

00:30:04.220 --> 00:30:06.470
were, and also what
our security model was,

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:08.720
and some of the threats
we wanted to mitigate.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:13.039
And serendipitously it worked
out that at the same time

00:30:13.039 --> 00:30:15.080
they were starting to
develop the concept of what

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:17.100
is now VPC service controls.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:19.820
So we spent a lot of
time in design sessions

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:22.220
with the product and
engineering teams,

00:30:22.220 --> 00:30:24.940
kind of understanding
what the roadmap was, sort

00:30:24.940 --> 00:30:27.800
of communicating what
our considerations were.

00:30:27.800 --> 00:30:29.900
And then were really
early adopters.

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:32.660
We obviously did our
validation testing, our

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:34.790
of form penetration testing.

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:37.190
And then were able to create
an environment that was

00:30:37.190 --> 00:30:39.490
Version 2 of what we have here.

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:41.240
But we tried to solve
some of the problems

00:30:41.240 --> 00:30:42.770
that Tony had highlighted.

00:30:42.770 --> 00:30:45.719
So up there, I'm
going to let Tony

00:30:45.719 --> 00:30:47.510
go into more detail
about some of the steps

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:51.047
that we took to address kind of
and some of those shortcomings.

00:30:51.047 --> 00:30:52.130
TONY WALKER: Thanks, Nick.

00:30:52.130 --> 00:30:54.810
So yeah as you see, the
diagram that's up here

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:57.060
is very similar to what we
had before with two really

00:30:57.060 --> 00:30:57.960
key differences.

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:01.800
So one, the GCP project is now
in its own secure perimeter.

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:04.530
And two, the project
is directly connected

00:31:04.530 --> 00:31:07.410
to Two Sigma via Private
Cloud Interconnect.

00:31:07.410 --> 00:31:11.460
Now this was-- this introduced
major benefits for us.

00:31:11.460 --> 00:31:13.800
First of all, users
could now safely

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:17.060
use all of the native services
directly from inside Two Sigma

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:18.060
from their home servers.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:20.980
We didn't have to maintain
these dual identities for them.

00:31:20.980 --> 00:31:23.510
So the IAM complexity
that I mentioned before

00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:25.221
was essentially removed.

00:31:25.221 --> 00:31:26.970
Additionally now that
we're going directly

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:30.820
over the private interconnect,
the upload and download problem

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:31.740
disappeared.

00:31:31.740 --> 00:31:33.750
You just could push
data with impunity

00:31:33.750 --> 00:31:35.990
because there was no
exfiltration risk.

00:31:35.990 --> 00:31:37.470
And they could
download over a pipe

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.011
which was much, much
bigger than what

00:31:39.011 --> 00:31:42.160
we were delivering via our
standard Internet access.

00:31:42.160 --> 00:31:44.227
Similar are some side benefits.

00:31:44.227 --> 00:31:46.560
By removing the need for these
bastion and home servers,

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:48.594
we actually reduced
the infrastructure

00:31:48.594 --> 00:31:50.760
that had to remain in the
project, so actually saved

00:31:50.760 --> 00:31:52.740
ourselves some money as well.

00:31:52.740 --> 00:31:55.380
So ultimately, this resulted
in a major velocity jump

00:31:55.380 --> 00:31:59.250
for the group that was
doing the research.

00:31:59.250 --> 00:32:00.690
And we quickly
realized that this

00:32:00.690 --> 00:32:01.920
was going to be something
that would be very

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:03.300
popular throughout the company.

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.700
So what we wanted to do was
to try and standardize this

00:32:05.700 --> 00:32:09.770
into a general platform that
we could roll out to anyone.

00:32:09.770 --> 00:32:12.210
Now before we did this,
there was one technical thing

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:15.000
we had to overcome,
and that is that to use

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:18.810
the secure perimeter access,
the perimeter project

00:32:18.810 --> 00:32:22.950
has to advertise a route
back to your internal network

00:32:22.950 --> 00:32:25.440
over which you can
access all the APIs.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:28.077
And this was fine for doing
this with a proof of concept

00:32:28.077 --> 00:32:30.660
that we had here, but as you can
imagine if we were doing this

00:32:30.660 --> 00:32:32.849
for potentially hundreds
of these projects

00:32:32.849 --> 00:32:34.890
we needed to find a way
that we could scale that.

00:32:34.890 --> 00:32:37.600
And Nick's going to talk
a bit more about that now

00:32:37.600 --> 00:32:38.850
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS: Thank you.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.460
Again we didn't want to
engineer something ourselves,

00:32:41.460 --> 00:32:44.820
so to a man with a hammer,
everything looks like a nail.

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:47.250
We'd had success previously
working with the Google team,

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:49.080
so we decided to
reach back out again

00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:52.600
and explain what our
new situation was.

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:54.300
And in this case,
again, it turned out

00:32:54.300 --> 00:32:56.520
that it was timely because
Google was thinking

00:32:56.520 --> 00:32:58.140
about the concept
of how to bridge

00:32:58.140 --> 00:33:00.510
multiple secure
perimeters together.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:04.350
At the time there was a product
or feature of VPC service

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:06.390
controls called Zone Bridges.

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:10.320
And really this led us to a
platform that you see now.

00:33:10.320 --> 00:33:12.780
At a very high level,
what zone bridges are is

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.000
you can take two
individual perimeters

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:16.710
and you can create a
bridge between them

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.290
such that the APIs can
be securely accessed

00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:21.400
from each individual project.

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.547
So you can define, essentially
multiple different parameters.

00:33:24.547 --> 00:33:26.130
And when you need
them to interconnect

00:33:26.130 --> 00:33:28.380
you can have them
interconnect, or you can

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:29.960
keep them completely distinct.

00:33:29.960 --> 00:33:32.460
So how we built this into an
architecture that really worked

00:33:32.460 --> 00:33:35.340
out for us was we
defined a single

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:38.460
what we called like a
gateway project for GCP.

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:42.280
And really what the role
of that was, was twofold.

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:45.960
The first role was to advertise
that route back to Two Sigma

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:48.990
that Tony was just talking
about to access a secure APIs.

00:33:48.990 --> 00:33:52.680
So that was our path into
access to the secure perimeters.

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:54.480
The other thing
that it did was also

00:33:54.480 --> 00:33:57.540
represent our internal
network and internal systems

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:59.679
itself as a source
secure perimeter,

00:33:59.679 --> 00:34:01.470
so that we could then
bridge that perimeter

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:05.020
with other parameters that
we wanted to ground access to

00:34:05.020 --> 00:34:07.560
and enable access to
native Google services

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:10.330
from within Two Sigma
in a secure fashion.

00:34:10.330 --> 00:34:13.139
So this really allowed us to
scale horizontally and create

00:34:13.139 --> 00:34:15.600
multiple different use
cases instead of having

00:34:15.600 --> 00:34:17.317
just that single project.

00:34:17.317 --> 00:34:18.900
There was one more
technical challenge

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:20.610
we had to deal with though.

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:22.920
And this is maybe
diving a little deeper.

00:34:22.920 --> 00:34:24.900
Again, we've kind
of hand-waved again

00:34:24.900 --> 00:34:28.620
how we got data, vendor data
securely into the project.

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:30.120
If you want to learn
more about that

00:34:30.120 --> 00:34:31.480
we can talk about it afterwards.

00:34:31.480 --> 00:34:32.917
But it's fairly complex.

00:34:32.917 --> 00:34:35.250
But the other challenge besides
that we had to deal with

00:34:35.250 --> 00:34:38.750
was when you access the
secure API endpoint,

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:40.500
it actually lives on
a different endpoint.

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:43.739
The endpoint is
restricted.googleapis.com.

00:34:43.739 --> 00:34:45.300
So you needed a
mechanism by which

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:48.870
you could remap any of the calls
like for example, Google Cloud

00:34:48.870 --> 00:34:50.469
Storage is normally storage.

00:34:50.469 --> 00:34:51.719
So googleapis.com.

00:34:51.719 --> 00:34:53.850
You needed a mechanism
to remap that

00:34:53.850 --> 00:34:57.300
to restricted.googleapis.com
for your clients.

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:59.610
At the time,
Google's private DNS

00:34:59.610 --> 00:35:03.840
didn't exist for us inside the
VPC so we couldn't use that.

00:35:03.840 --> 00:35:06.670
And we also needed a solution
for our internal network.

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:08.386
So again, we went and
did some research.

00:35:08.386 --> 00:35:10.260
This time we didn't
trouble Google with this.

00:35:10.260 --> 00:35:12.180
We solved this one ourselves.

00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:14.880
But we learned that within
our own internal DNS

00:35:14.880 --> 00:35:18.150
infrastructure, there was
a mechanism for this called

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:20.070
response policy zones.

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:22.020
So RPZ is what it's known as.

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:24.000
We implemented that
and we were good to go.

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:25.760
Any requests for
Google APIs we're now

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:29.580
hitting the restricted endpoints
and our users are good.

00:35:29.580 --> 00:35:31.559
TONY WALKER: So
in conclusion this

00:35:31.559 --> 00:35:32.850
was a real game changer for us.

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:34.830
Having private
access to the APIs

00:35:34.830 --> 00:35:37.230
directly from our
own internal network

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:39.010
enabled researchers
to get up and running,

00:35:39.010 --> 00:35:41.370
and access to the native
tools that we ultimately want

00:35:41.370 --> 00:35:43.470
to roll out across other teams.

00:35:43.470 --> 00:35:45.966
And unsurprisingly,
after we did this,

00:35:45.966 --> 00:35:47.340
we found many
internal teams were

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:50.780
coming to us wanting to make
use of what this capability gave

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:51.390
them.

00:35:51.390 --> 00:35:53.520
And we expect this to
continue as Google rolls out

00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:56.137
more and more services
into VPC service controls.

00:35:56.137 --> 00:35:57.720
So if you're interested,
as Nick said,

00:35:57.720 --> 00:35:58.770
if you're interested
in hearing anything

00:35:58.770 --> 00:36:00.210
more about this
at a deeper level,

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:01.800
come talk to us afterwards.

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:02.509
We'll be outside.

00:36:02.509 --> 00:36:04.425
And if you think any of
it sounds interesting,

00:36:04.425 --> 00:36:04.990
we're hiring.

00:36:08.416 --> 00:36:09.790
NICHOLAS ARVANITIS:
So with that,

00:36:09.790 --> 00:36:12.230
will hand over to Vineet to
talk about Google's partner

00:36:12.230 --> 00:36:12.880
ecosystem.

00:36:14.982 --> 00:36:15.940
VINEET BHAN: Thank you.

00:36:20.170 --> 00:36:22.210
So you guys heard a
lot of great stuff

00:36:22.210 --> 00:36:24.860
that our Google
has been building.

00:36:24.860 --> 00:36:28.240
The key security concept and
features that you can use.

00:36:28.240 --> 00:36:29.920
In addition to
what Google builds,

00:36:29.920 --> 00:36:32.620
there are partners, security
companies, that all of you

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:34.630
have been working
for years and years.

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:37.090
We also work closely with
those security companies

00:36:37.090 --> 00:36:38.980
to make sure their
solutions can complement

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:42.290
what Google can do so you can
seamlessly use those products.

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:45.400
So I will look over how we
are working with partners

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:47.110
and how they
complement what Google

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:49.360
does in terms of security.

00:36:49.360 --> 00:36:51.490
So the most important
concept to understand first

00:36:51.490 --> 00:36:54.070
is the shared security model,
I wanted to re-emphasize that

00:36:54.070 --> 00:36:55.290
at the start.

00:36:55.290 --> 00:36:58.270
So security at the core
infrastructure level

00:36:58.270 --> 00:37:00.730
will be something that
Google will provide for you

00:37:00.730 --> 00:37:01.990
at the infrastructure level.

00:37:01.990 --> 00:37:04.750
But for your services,
your projects,

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:07.620
you have to think about
how you would secure it.

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:11.290
Leveraging the technology that
Google is building and layering

00:37:11.290 --> 00:37:13.160
on partner products
on top of that.

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:15.340
So you have to
think about clearly

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:17.380
in terms of what things
are covered by Google

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:18.970
at the infrastructure level.

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:22.240
What is your responsibility
and how do you

00:37:22.240 --> 00:37:26.062
cover that and have a
security policy to do that.

00:37:26.062 --> 00:37:28.270
So there are a lot of Google
products as I mentioned.

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.269
There are also partner
products that complement.

00:37:30.269 --> 00:37:31.390
This is an I-chart.

00:37:31.390 --> 00:37:34.360
I wouldn't cover all the boxes.

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:37.060
But there are a couple
of areas or categories

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:38.050
that I would emphasize.

00:37:38.050 --> 00:37:40.900
For example, the next-gen
firewall category.

00:37:40.900 --> 00:37:42.730
They have been around
for a long time.

00:37:42.730 --> 00:37:44.700
Most companies use them.

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:46.360
Its a kind of category
of products that

00:37:46.360 --> 00:37:48.070
complement to what Google do.

00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:51.450
And you know we can think
of hardware security

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:53.500
module as an example
of another category

00:37:53.500 --> 00:37:57.470
that companies use especially
in the banking sector.

00:37:57.470 --> 00:37:59.740
So you can use those
products in addition

00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:04.407
to what Google provides natively
to meet different requirements

00:38:04.407 --> 00:38:04.990
that you have.

00:38:04.990 --> 00:38:06.500
It could be regulatory
requirements.

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:10.602
Could be you're comfortable
using a product from a partner

00:38:10.602 --> 00:38:12.060
and you want to
continue and extend

00:38:12.060 --> 00:38:14.710
that product into
your GCP deployments,

00:38:14.710 --> 00:38:16.360
you can absolutely do that.

00:38:16.360 --> 00:38:20.080
So think about how you kind of
layer on the partner products

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:23.020
with what Google provides.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:26.830
So other just kind of sharing
kind of the ecosystem we

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:28.150
have with partners.

00:38:28.150 --> 00:38:30.490
Over the last one
year we have grown it

00:38:30.490 --> 00:38:32.510
4x in terms of
security companies.

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:35.470
So a huge increase
in terms of security,

00:38:35.470 --> 00:38:37.840
leading names in security
who have solutions.

00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:41.920
A broad category of
partners so and pretty much

00:38:41.920 --> 00:38:44.560
all the big names in security
that you can think of.

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:48.580
Any product that you use in
security works with GCP now.

00:38:48.580 --> 00:38:50.854
So you can think
about which categories

00:38:50.854 --> 00:38:53.020
are relevant in your business,
which products you're

00:38:53.020 --> 00:38:54.820
comfortable using and
you can absolutely

00:38:54.820 --> 00:38:56.620
go use those products.

00:38:56.620 --> 00:38:59.830
And most of these products
are listed on the Google GCP

00:38:59.830 --> 00:39:00.470
marketplace.

00:39:00.470 --> 00:39:02.303
So you can look at the
market based products

00:39:02.303 --> 00:39:04.750
that are available or
work with the partners

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:08.709
and ask them know how
do they support GCP.

00:39:08.709 --> 00:39:10.750
So I will just not go
through all the categories.

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:13.404
I will just double click
on a few categories that

00:39:13.404 --> 00:39:15.070
are very important,
especially for folks

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:17.920
who are you're new to GCP.

00:39:17.920 --> 00:39:21.060
So with the next-gen firewalls
that I mentioned before.

00:39:21.060 --> 00:39:24.250
App level of visibility, the
hybrid connectivity from cloud,

00:39:24.250 --> 00:39:27.940
to your on-prem deployments,
are very common use cases

00:39:27.940 --> 00:39:29.830
that we see.

00:39:29.830 --> 00:39:33.430
Pretty much you know all major
next-generation firewalls

00:39:33.430 --> 00:39:34.540
are supported on GCP.

00:39:34.540 --> 00:39:38.380
And it's not only
supporting them

00:39:38.380 --> 00:39:41.260
and having their firewall
run as a VM in GCP,

00:39:41.260 --> 00:39:43.840
we're working very closely
with leading security companies

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:46.450
to build next generation
of security solutions.

00:39:46.450 --> 00:39:50.470
So are not just match what
used to happen on-prem,

00:39:50.470 --> 00:39:52.180
but how do we make
security better

00:39:52.180 --> 00:39:54.040
by working with
security companies.

00:39:54.040 --> 00:39:55.960
How do we do it in a
more scalable fashion,

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:57.889
more automatable fashion.

00:39:57.889 --> 00:40:00.430
So a lot of interesting stuff
a lot of interesting innovation

00:40:00.430 --> 00:40:02.760
happening with these partners.

00:40:02.760 --> 00:40:05.050
And one key concept
is that works

00:40:05.050 --> 00:40:07.000
very well with these
next-gen firewalls

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:09.310
is a shared VPC concept.

00:40:09.310 --> 00:40:10.810
So if you guys
haven't looked at it,

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:14.440
it's a very seamless way
of routing all your traffic

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:17.260
to next-gen firewall, having
controls at the host project

00:40:17.260 --> 00:40:18.040
level.

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:20.080
And the service
projects will be routed,

00:40:20.080 --> 00:40:22.960
all the traffic will be
routed to your host project.

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:25.360
So it's a very
unique Google feature

00:40:25.360 --> 00:40:28.420
that works very well
with a next-gen firewall.

00:40:28.420 --> 00:40:30.890
I had been talking to
customers and partners,

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:33.420
and the comments I got
was this is closest

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:35.890
we have seen to
getting the controls we

00:40:35.890 --> 00:40:38.460
used to have on-prem and in
a public cloud environment.

00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:41.020
So if you haven't looked at
it, please do look at it.

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:44.870
It's a very strong
and useful feature.

00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:47.680
So next category I want to talk
about was application security.

00:40:47.680 --> 00:40:50.680
So as you think about
how do I protect

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:53.270
against application
level DDoS attacks,

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.940
WAIF kind of functionality.

00:40:55.940 --> 00:40:58.150
Ines mentioned
about Cloud Armor.

00:40:58.150 --> 00:41:00.620
So we have lot of
flexibility in this category.

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:03.764
As an example, you could use
Cloud Armor, the native rules

00:41:03.764 --> 00:41:04.930
that we have on Cloud Armor.

00:41:04.930 --> 00:41:08.740
You could have partner rules
that partners are generating

00:41:08.740 --> 00:41:10.780
and ingest them
into Cloud Armor.

00:41:10.780 --> 00:41:14.560
You could have a DNS based WAIF
or DNS based application DDoS

00:41:14.560 --> 00:41:15.394
protection solution.

00:41:15.394 --> 00:41:16.601
So you don't deploy anything.

00:41:16.601 --> 00:41:18.280
It's deployed outside
of Google Cloud,

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:20.450
or you could use a
VM based solution.

00:41:20.450 --> 00:41:22.250
So there are a lot
of flexibility,

00:41:22.250 --> 00:41:23.770
a lot of options available.

00:41:23.770 --> 00:41:26.500
Depending on your requirements
or your preferences,

00:41:26.500 --> 00:41:30.850
you can pick any of the
options that are available.

00:41:30.850 --> 00:41:33.430
So next category that's
kind of very unique to cloud

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:35.850
is the compliance in
the Cloud category.

00:41:35.850 --> 00:41:37.660
These are Cloud
native solutions.

00:41:37.660 --> 00:41:42.400
Companies like RedLock, Dome9
and others, that will help you.

00:41:42.400 --> 00:41:44.620
Once you are deployed, you
want to get a quick check

00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:46.990
on where do you stand in
terms of your PCI compliance.

00:41:46.990 --> 00:41:49.130
So they can go in, do
a quick check and say,

00:41:49.130 --> 00:41:51.327
hey you're compliant,
or these rules

00:41:51.327 --> 00:41:53.410
need to be changed to make
you more PCI compliant.

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:55.610
So this makes your
life much easier.

00:41:55.610 --> 00:41:57.340
So if you compare
this with on-prem role

00:41:57.340 --> 00:42:00.850
where everything had to be
done you know largely manually,

00:42:00.850 --> 00:42:04.240
these are the kind of
partners who can bring in

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:06.052
with just creating
a service account,

00:42:06.052 --> 00:42:07.510
can generate reports
within minutes

00:42:07.510 --> 00:42:08.840
to tell you where you are.

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:10.600
So this helps to get
you more compliant.

00:42:10.600 --> 00:42:15.310
Another layer of checks
that you can layer in.

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:17.170
So other key concept
I wanted to share

00:42:17.170 --> 00:42:18.970
was in the Cloud
Security Command Center

00:42:18.970 --> 00:42:20.770
is a great tool that
Google has built

00:42:20.770 --> 00:42:23.830
that you guys may have seen
some of the sessions regarding

00:42:23.830 --> 00:42:25.760
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:29.160
But this is one place where all
the thread data, all the kind

00:42:29.160 --> 00:42:32.380
of visibility of what services,
what kind of resources

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:33.730
you are using.

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:36.510
And you can consolidate
all the thread data,

00:42:36.510 --> 00:42:41.320
all the analysis Google runs on
security in the command center,

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:43.460
and also at partner
products on top of that.

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:45.220
So this will be your
one place in GCP

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:47.650
where you can get all
the Google signals,

00:42:47.650 --> 00:42:49.150
you will get all
the partner signals

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:52.160
to see in one dashboard
what's going on.

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:53.695
So this is a great innovation.

00:42:53.695 --> 00:42:55.070
It simplifies your life.

00:42:55.070 --> 00:42:58.254
So one place to get
a security poster.

00:42:58.254 --> 00:42:59.920
And we're working
closely with companies

00:42:59.920 --> 00:43:01.795
to kind of take it to
the next level as well.

00:43:01.795 --> 00:43:05.290
So once you have
the visibility, how

00:43:05.290 --> 00:43:08.830
do you make it easier for you
to kind of react with the data

00:43:08.830 --> 00:43:10.956
that you have.

00:43:10.956 --> 00:43:14.050
With that, I would
like to close on

00:43:14.050 --> 00:43:18.010
with just rehashing I think what
we discussed in this session.

00:43:18.010 --> 00:43:21.340
I think about when you're
thinking about security as Ines

00:43:21.340 --> 00:43:25.060
mentioned, think about
security you know right

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:30.430
from the perimeter and from your
edge, to code off your network.

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:32.720
And when you're
creating security rules,

